{
  "name": "Webhook Dedupe · 2 Receiver (Data Tables)",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Webhook Dedupe · Receiver on Data Tables\n`POST dedupe/data-table` with a JSON body that has an `id`. Same behaviour as the Postgres receiver, with no database of your own.\n\nA Data Table has no unique constraint, so \"look, then insert\" lets several runs through. This one turns it around: **insert first, then look**. Every run adds its own row, then reads the row with the lowest `id` for the key. The run whose row that is has the claim. The others delete their row again.\n\nThe first row stays as the record of the event. A failed or stale claim is taken over with one conditional update on that row, decided by the `owner` in the row that comes back.\n\nSettings are at the top of the **Event** node. Put your own call in **Do the work**.",
        "width": 640,
        "height": 380,
        "color": 4
      },
      "id": "d587606f-827e-4011-85ae-4953ec158643",
      "name": "About",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -40,
        -440
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### The conflict path\n- **Duplicate: done** answers `200`. The event was handled, the sender can stop.\n- **Duplicate: in progress** answers `409`. Another run holds the claim and has not finished. A `2xx` here would tell the sender to stop while that run can still crash. With `409` it retries later and then finds the event done, failed or stale.\n- **Failed** answers `500` and frees the claim, so the next delivery runs again.\n- **Superseded**: this run was so slow that a retry took the claim over. It may not mark the event done.\n\nIf your sender gives up after a few non-2xx answers, change `409` to `200` and accept that a crash in that moment needs a manual replay.",
        "width": 640,
        "height": 300,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "3633316e-9de2-4850-8f75-50cfb077c978",
      "name": "Conflict",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        1980,
        -440
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "httpMethod": "POST",
        "path": "dedupe/data-table",
        "authentication": "headerAuth",
        "responseMode": "responseNode",
        "options": {}
      },
      "id": "f9400583-1a80-4299-804b-0fe08b9686a5",
      "name": "Webhook",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.webhook",
      "typeVersion": 2,
      "position": [
        0,
        0
      ],
      "webhookId": "c112aa91-afed-487f-8dc0-7492cf1b0912",
      "credentials": {
        "httpHeaderAuth": {
          "id": "webhookDedupeKey",
          "name": "Webhook Dedupe Key"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "jsCode": "// Settings.\nconst SOURCE = 'demo'; // scope of the key: one value per sender, so ids of two senders cannot collide\nconst LEASE_SECONDS = 900; // a claim still \"processing\" after this long may be taken over. Set it longer than your slowest run.\n\nconst body = $input.first().json.body ?? {};\n// Where the sender puts its event id. Change this line for your sender.\nconst id = body.id === undefined || body.id === null ? '' : String(body.id).trim();\nconst hex = () => Math.floor(Math.random() * 0x100000000).toString(16).padStart(8, '0');\nreturn [\n  {\n    json: {\n      event_key: id ? `${SOURCE}:${id}` : '',\n      // The run token. Every later write checks it, so a run that lost its\n      // claim cannot mark the event done.\n      owner: `${$execution.id}-${hex()}${hex()}`,\n      lease_seconds: LEASE_SECONDS,\n      event: body,\n    },\n  },\n];"
      },
